phase P3
title Conformance rewrite
description
    **Class:** not readily verifiable.

    **Kind:** verification-suite.

    **Goal:** a new, clean conformance suite at production quality in
    `packages/flow-state-v3/conformance/`, running against the prototype runtime (round 14) and,
    from P4, the production runtime, through one Port; one oracle per guarantee with its mutants;
    the old suite's fixtures, scenarios, reference model and lies copied, its runner, lock, shims
    and text formats rewritten.

    **Verified by:**

    - the new suite green on round 14 with the old suite's verdicts;
    - null 0 of N;
    - falsify (every oracle kills its mutants, each for the expected reason);
    - an audit and re-audit by a lead that did not build it (skill:policy-testing#audit-a-suite);
    - its coverage record, in @plan.Runtime's verification once the user says yes to that Plan edit.

    **One iteration:** one Task: grow the suite from its Spec, run it on round 14 and the null
    runtime, run its mutants, get the hourly review, gather findings by root cause.

    **Exit criteria:**

    - the suite passes on round 14;
    - it kills every mutant the old suite kills;
    - every oracle kills its own mutants;
    - it passes its audit and re-audit;
    - its fast gates run in `nub run verify`;
    - its coverage record exists;
    - the user's speed targets hold (Q4 A, 2026-11-08):
      - the suite in one process at most 15 s,
      - peak RSS per suite process at most 600 MB,
      - the builder's fast loop at most 30 s wall,
      - every final-commit gate at up to 8 in parallel at most 10 min wall,
      - or the gap reported with its limiter.

    **False pass:** agreeing with the old suite because it was copied, weaknesses included.

    **Caught by:** the audit on the new suite, whose auditor writes its own mutants from the Specs.

    **Gates:**

    - falsify, which rejects the null runtime and every registered mutant
      (`packages/flow-state-v3/conformance/`, each oracle's mutants file);
    - `nub run check`, which rejects a planted lint violation.

    **Budget:** the Epic's Budget section, row P3 (25 rounds, 5 used).

    **Failure limit:** 2.

    **Stops:**

    - a guarantee change (a Spec Invariant added, dropped or changed);
    - a guarantee that can't be met.

    **User's read:**

    - the suite layout (decided in round 1),
    - each Spec revision,
    - each changed expected result with its reason.

    **Draws from:**

    - skill:policy-verification (prove a suite; the coverage record);
    - skill:policy-testing (audit a suite);
    - skill:to-spec for the suite's own Spec;
    - skill:policy-convergence at the threshold;
    - skill:review for the hourly reviews;
    - skill:check-benchmark on the speed numbers.
comment 2026-11-09T13:30:00Z
    Rounds 1 to 5 done before the move to this Epic (`976ca17c` to `bc8dffba`):

    - Port with round-14 and null adapters, fake world by host, DSL, runner with `--parallel` and
      falsify;
    - oracles three-channels, attempt-identity, one-delivery, lane-cells;
    - suite 61/61, null 0/61, falsify 25/25, `nub run check` at zero.

    **Unreviewed:** `3097ea3c` and `bc8dffba`, the run's first cadence review.

    **Work list:** the README's guarantee map and items 1 to 9.
tasks
    T4
        title       The suite's own Spec, Conformance.spec
        status      review
        priority    high
        labels      [not-readily-verifiable, verification-suite]
        description
            Write `packages/flow-state-v3/conformance/Conformance.spec` with skill:to-spec, next to
            the suite (README item 7):

            - the Port exposes no runtime internals and any runtime implementing it gets the same
              verdicts;
            - expected results come from a cited Spec rule, never from running the runtime;
            - every check has a mutant it kills;
            - the null runtime fails every case;
            - runs are deterministic from a seed;
            - the verdict format and the old-suite comparison.

            The README keeps the how-to.

            - **May change:** the new Spec, `workflow/spec.index`, the README.
            - **Evidence:** this Task's comments.
            - **False pass:** a Spec restating the code's current behaviour → caught by
              skill:check-spec by an agent that did not write it.
            - **Gates:** `workflow check`.
            - **Budget:** 1 round.
            - **Stops:** none.
            - **User's read:** the Spec's guarantees.
        acceptance
            The Spec exists, `workflow check` passes, and an independent skill:check-spec finds
            no gap in its contract.
        comment     2026-11-09T16:20:54Z
            Written: @spec.Conformance (`workflow/specs/conformance/Conformance.spec`), status proposed, with 
            skill:to-spec.
            11 Requirements, 30 Invariants, 23 tests, 7 open questions.

            **Location changed.** The description says next to the suite; the user has since ruled that Specs live in 
            the
            workflow folder ("it should just be in the workflow repo", 2026-11-09). README work list item 7 now points
            there.

            **What it guarantees** (derived from the suite's purpose and the README's decisions, not from today's code):

            - R1 to R3: the Port carries public behaviour only (one settling exception, `pending`); verdicts never 
              depend
              on which runtime runs; adapters translate and never fill reports in from suite facts, outside stated 
              limits.
            - R4, R5: oracles anchor reports to suite facts; every case and oracle cites core Spec rules that resolve;
              no expected value comes from running a runtime.
            - R6, R7, R10: every check (an oracle, or a case expectation that alone checks an Invariant) has a mutant
              killed by that check; null fails every case by a stated failure; falsify's pass conditions.
            - R8: verdicts are a function of code, case and world seed; order and process split change nothing.
            - R9: one verdict per requested case, machine and human forms, exit codes.
            - R11: the old-suite comparison: the new suite kills every mutant the old one kills on ported cases; every
              difference has a reading.

            **Agent decisions for the user's read** (pending): a consistency-only check is never an Invariant's only
            check (R4); a thrown defect does not count as failing on null (R7); a separate `Defect` verdict kind,
            proposed name (R9).

            **Finding.** R7 does not hold today: `suite --runtime null` gives 0/61, but 29 of those failures are a
            thrown `NoHeldRequest`, which `dsl/case.ts` calls a scenario bug, never a verdict (Q2). The P3 evidence
            "null 0/61" overstates what null proves.

            **Tests:** 9 name existing checks (falsify's oracle-has-a-mutant, control, judge, exactly-once and null
            lines; `runCase`'s seeds; `report`; `sliceVerdicts`; the Oracle type); the rest say "phase 3 writes this"
            or "phase 4 writes this".

            **For skill:check-spec:** whether any Invariant restates current behaviour rather than purpose (the
            Task's false pass); whether R6's definition of a check (oracles plus case-only Invariants) is the right
            granularity, given Q5 (per-rule mutants); whether R2's "stated limit" escape hatch is bounded enough;
            whether `derives_from [@task.V3.T4]` is the right provenance, since no Plan outcome covers the suite.

            **Sidecars:** none started. The check and mutant inventory was read directly (4 oracles, 25 mutants in
            `oracles/*.lies.ts`) and the null run done by the lead, so no sidecar result to log.

            Gates: `workflow index`, `workflow format --write`, `workflow check` valid, `workflow refs --dangling` none.
